--- body ---
## Context
Every reviewed merged PR leaves a post-merge annex: a ~30-line
`memory/journal/YYYY/*-review-attribution-prNNNN.md` (130 so far), a
`memory/evidence/` file, and a roar capture commit. The record is already
complete in the PR body's `attribution` block when `/merge` runs (ticket 1151),
so roar copies forge text into a file after the fact. The author called the
merge-time extraction and capture burdensome (2026-10-27 discussion).

Alternative rejected: `erg log` lines on the closed ticket. That needs a
strict one-ticket-per-PR rule. The log grammar is single-line, so the
30-line record either becomes 30 entries per ticket or a lossy summary that
breaks the shared-catch anchors `scripts/attribution_query.py` scores on.
`.erg` files are also plaintext, with no equivalent of the `.age` audience.

## Proposal
`/merge` passes the PR body's attribution block as the merge commit body
(`gh pr merge --body`; harness extension point for other forges). The commit
date replaces the `pending` swap. `attribution_query.py` reads
`git log --merges` blocks plus the existing journal files (no migration).
Roar's attribution capture (one call since ticket 1242) goes away; the seats join moves before merge (into
the `/gaze` closing or `/merge` step 0).

## Author decisions (needs-human)
1. **Private audience.** A merge commit is plaintext. Do private projects keep
   the encrypted journal path, or does forge-level privacy suffice? It
   suffices unless the repo may be published later.
2. **Late appends.** Body edits after merge no longer reach the record, so the
   block must be complete at merge. Should `/merge` refuse when it is not,
   instead of today's warning?

## Exit criteria
- [ ] A merged PR's attribution block is the merge commit body, read back after merge
- [ ] `attribution_query.py` reports identical scores on the 130 journal records before and after, and counts merge-commit records once
- [ ] Roar's capture runbook no longer writes review-attribution journal files for public projects
- [ ] The private-audience path follows the author's decision
- [ ] `make check` passes
